TEST 30681 -- Test IPv6 multicast routes time out once traffic stops
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-24 (device clock 03:07:44 -> 03:18:19 UTC), tb470
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk", sole PIM-SMv6 router and RP
Setup:  as 38144.log (same session): source eth3 -> port3.0.9 vlan30 (RP fd32:b1f0:dff8:d703::10);
        receiver eth1 -> port3.0.13 vlan20; receiver eth2 -> IE520-sa -> sa3 -> vlan1.
        Receivers are tb470 kernel MLDv2 joins; the source is frames from eth3 (src
        fd32:b1f0:dff8:d703::1); counts are taken on the wire.
        (Replaces the 2026-09-22 UNMEASURED "needs more hosts".)

VERDICT: PASS -- every entry the test's traffic created is gone 10 minutes after the reports
         and the stream stop, from both the control plane and hardware.

STEP 1 -- "Configure a PIM-SMv6 network and confirm the DUT is routing IPv6 multicast traffic"
          -> entries visible in show ipv6 mroute / pim sparse-mode mroute / platform table
          ipmulti / hsl fib
  eth1 and eth2 join ff1e::1:700; 40 frames from eth3  ->  eth1 40/40, eth2 40/40
  03:07:44 UTC:
    show ipv6 mroute:
      (fd32:b1f0:dff8:d703::1, ff1e::1:700), uptime 00:00:06, Owner PIM-SMv6, Flags: TF
        Incoming interface: vlan30   Outgoing interface list: vlan1, vlan20
    show ipv6 pim sparse-mode mroute:   (*,G) 4, (S,G) 8, (S,G,rpt) 2
      (*, ff1e::1:700)                           Upstream JOINED, Local 2
      (fd32:b1f0:dff8:d703::1, ff1e::1:700)      SPT bit 1, JOINED, Outgoing 2
      (fd32:b1f0:dff8:d703::1, ff1e::1:700, rpt) NOT PRUNED, Outgoing 2
    show hsl fib, IPv6 L3 MC Tree:
      Group (ff1e::1:700) Source (fd32:b1f0:dff8:d703::1)  L2 L3 Entry, Mirror To CPU: No
        L3 IIf = 330; L3 outgoing 301 (vlan1), 320 (vlan20)
      (*,G) Total 5, (S,G) Forwarding 3, MC Total 8
    show ipv6 mld groups:  ff1e::1:700 on vlan20 (port3.0.13) and vlan1 (sa3)
    show platform table ipmulti:  only the "IPv4 Multicast Route Table" (224.0.0.0/24 on each
      member); this platform's table has no IPv6 section (see 38144.log), so the IPv6 hardware
