STEP 2 -- "Stop the MLD Reports and IPv6 Multicast streams and leave the DUT for 10 minutes"
          -> no multicast entries in the same four outputs
  Joins closed and stream stopped at 15:07:56 NZST; re-read 623 s later (03:18:19 UTC):
    show ipv6 mroute:                  EMPTY (the table header only)
    show ipv6 pim sparse-mode mroute:  (S,G) 0, (S,G,rpt) 0; (*,G) 3 -- see below
    show hsl fib, IPv6 L3 MC Tree:     (S,G) Forwarding 0; no ff1e::1:700 entry of any kind;
                                       (*,G) 3, MC Total 3 -- see below
    show ipv6 mld groups:              ff1e::1:700 gone from both VLANs
    show platform table ipmulti:       unchanged (the IPv4 224.0.0.0/24 link entry only)
  Every (S,G) in the DUT aged out, not only ff1e::1:700: the earlier cases' ff1e::1:201, :202,
  :300, :500, :501 and ff3e::1:400 went too.

  The three (*,G) entries that REMAIN each have an owner the test never stopped. They are not
  routes left behind by stopped traffic:
    (*, ff1e::1:500)  vlan20 -- `ipv6 mld static-group ff1e::1:500` from 11736 is still
                      configured; mld groups shows its Expires as "stopped" (static).
    (*, ff05::2)      vlan20 + vlan1 -- tb470's own kernel keeps reporting this group on eth1
                      and eth2 (Last Reporter = the two NICs' link-locals, expiry refreshed).
    (*, ff02::1:2)    link-scope DHCPv6 group, local.
  Each hsl fib (*,G) is an L2 entry for one of these same groups.

NOTES
  - The IE520-sa's MLD snooping kept sa3 as a dynamically learned router port on vlan1
    throughout (show ipv6 mld snooping mrouter, 60 s after the stop).
